Use the **`-p`** flag:

```bash
diff -p file1.c file2.c
```

This tells diff to show which C function each hunk of changes is in. For example, the output will look like:

```
*** old.c
--- new.c
***************
*** 10,15 ****
  int foo(int x) {
      return x + 1;
  }
! int bar(int x) {
      return x * 2;
  }
--- 10,15 ----
  int foo(int x) {
      return x + 1;
  }
! int baz(int x) {
      return x * 2;
  }
```

The function name appears in the hunk header (the line starting with `***` or `---` that includes the function context).

**For unified diff format** (more common in patches), combine it with `-u`:

```bash
diff -u -p file1.c file2.c
```

This gives you unified diff output with the enclosing function name in each hunk header:

```
--- old.c
+++ new.c
@@ -10,7 +10,7 @@ int bar(int x) {
     return x * 2;
 }
```

The `int bar(int x) {` part after the `@@` line is the function context provided by `-p`.

**Note:** This only works for C/C++ code. Diff uses heuristics to recognize function definitions (lines ending with `{` that look like function signatures).

